 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Read axis labels and title",
   "Check the scale on each axis",
   "Read values from bar tops or plotted points",
   "Spot the trend: rising, falling, or fluctuating",
   "Compare: highest, lowest, range"
  ],
  "title": "Bar Charts & Line Graphs",
  "content": "<p><strong>Bar charts</strong> use rectangular bars to compare quantities across categories. Each bar's height represents a value — the taller the bar, the higher the value. They are ideal for comparing discrete data such as rainfall totals for different months or population figures for different countries.</p><p><strong>Line graphs</strong> show how data changes over time. Points are plotted and connected with a line, making it easy to identify trends, peaks and troughs. They are essential in geography for showing climate data, population growth and changing river levels.</p><p><strong>Choosing scales:</strong> Your y-axis should start at zero (unless there's a good reason not to) and use regular intervals that make reading values easy — multiples of 5, 10, 50 or 100 work well. The x-axis should clearly label each category (bar chart) or time period (line graph).</p><p><strong>Compound bar charts</strong> stack different categories within each bar, showing both individual and total values. <strong>Dual-axis graphs</strong> plot two different variables (e.g. temperature and rainfall) on the same chart using different y-axes — these are called <strong>climate graphs</strong> in geography.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Question:</strong> A bar chart shows average monthly rainfall in Manchester. The bars for June, July and August reach 70mm, 65mm and 75mm. What is the total summer rainfall?</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the three summer months: June, July, August.</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Read each value: 70 + 65 + 75.</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate the total: 70 + 65 + 75 = 210mm.</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> 210mm</p>"
 },
